      "text": "## Bottom line\n\n**This is a strong offer, but I would negotiate rather than accept immediately. I would not walk away based on compensation alone.**\n\nAssuming the RSUs vest evenly and the stock price stays flat:\n\n| Component | Annual value |\n|---|---:|\n| Base | $235,000 |\n| Target bonus | $47,000 |\n| RSUs | $270,000 |\n| **Target annual compensation** | **$552,000** |\n\nThat is **$2.208 million over four years** before refresh grants and stock-price changes.\n\nFor context, Levels.fyi currently reports approximately **$350,000 median**, **$480,000 at the 75th percentile**, and **$645,000 at the 90th percentile** for Bay Area Senior Product Managers. The comparison is imperfect because company level definitions vary, but your $552,000 package is clearly competitive\u2014likely somewhere between the broad-market 75th and 90th percentiles. ([levels.fyi](https://www.levels.fyi/en-US/t/product-manager/levels/senior/locations/san-francisco-bay-area?utm_source=openai))\n\n## Where the offer is strong\u2014and where there is room\n\n### Base: good, but negotiable\n\nThe stated range is $183,000\u2013$271,000:\n\n- Midpoint: **$227,000**\n- Offer: **$235,000**\n- Offer is only **$8,000 above midpoint**\n- It is approximately **59% of the way through the range**\n- There is **$36,000 of disclosed headroom**\n\nCalifornia\u2019s posted range is supposed to represent the employer\u2019s good-faith estimate of what it expects to pay upon hire, and it generally does not have to include bonus or equity. Thus, $271,000 may not be obtainable for you, but it is legitimate evidence that $235,000 is not the limit. ([dir.ca.gov](https://dir.ca.gov/dlse/california_equal_pay_act.htm?utm_source=openai))\n\n### Equity: excellent, but risky\n\nA $1.08 million four-year RSU grant is the standout portion of the offer. But its actual value depends on:\n\n- Share price when the grant is converted into shares\n- Share price on each vesting date\n- Vesting schedule\n- Remaining employed through each vest\n- Trading-window restrictions\n\nFor example, ignoring timing differences:\n\n- At a 20% stock decline: annual equity \u2248 **$216,000**, total compensation \u2248 **$498,000**\n- At the stated grant value: annual equity = **$270,000**, total compensation = **$552,000**\n- At a 20% stock increase: annual equity \u2248 **$324,000**, total compensation \u2248 **$606,000**\n\n### Refresh grants: value them at zero for the decision\n\nThe recruiter handled this appropriately: refresh grants commonly stack with existing grants, but their timing and amount are normally discretionary. ([carta.com](https://carta.com/learn/equity/compensation/equity-refresh/?utm_source=openai))\n\nTreat refreshes as possible upside\u2014not part of the offer\u2014unless the company puts a minimum grant or explicit commitment in writing.\n\n## What I would ask for\n\nMake one confident package request:\n\n- **Base: $255,000**\n- **New-hire RSUs: $1,300,000**\n- **Sign-on bonus: $75,000**\n- Keep the 20% target bonus\n\nThat opening request would produce:\n\n- Ongoing target annual compensation:  \n  $255,000 + $51,000 + $325,000 = **$631,000**\n- First-year target compensation including sign-on: **$706,000**\n\nThat is an aggressive but credible opening\u2014not necessarily what you expect them to grant in full.\n\n### A good negotiated outcome\n\nInternally, I would consider this a strong landing point:\n\n- **Base: $250,000**\n- **RSUs: $1,200,000**\n- **Sign-on: $50,000**\n- 20% target bonus\n\nThat would equal:\n\n- Ongoing annual target compensation: **$600,000**\n- First-year target compensation: **$650,000**\n\nEven a result such as **$245,000 base plus $1.2 million equity**, or the original package plus a **$50,000\u2013$75,000 sign-on**, would be meaningful progress.\n\n## How to deliver the request\n\nSay something like:\n\n> I\u2019m genuinely excited about the role, team, and scope. Because I wasn\u2019t actively looking and would be leaving a position where I\u2019m happy, the compensation needs to make the move sufficiently compelling. Given my experience, the scope of the role, and the posted base range, I\u2019d like to see whether you can get the package to $255,000 base, $1.3 million in new-hire RSUs, and a $75,000 sign-on bonus. If there are constraints on individual components, I\u2019m open to discussing how to structure the package.\n\nThen stop talking and let the recruiter respond.\n\nDo **not** give them your lower acceptable package in the same conversation. Your landing target is for you, not the recruiter.\n\n## How to prioritize the components\n\nMy default priority would be:\n\n1. **Base salary**\n2. **New-hire equity**\n3. **Sign-on bonus**\n4. **Refresh promises**\n\nBase is recurring, increases the dollar value of the 20% bonus, and usually affects subsequent raises. Equity has the biggest dollar upside but carries stock and retention risk. Sign-on cash is certain, although it may include a repayment provision if you leave within a year.\n\nIf you are forfeiting near-term Varrowby equity or bonus, change the priority:\n\n1. Ask for a **specific make-whole sign-on or RSU increase**\n2. Equity\n3. Base\n\nFor example:\n\n> I will forfeit approximately $X of compensation scheduled to vest or pay over the next 12 months. I\u2019d like the sign-on component to cover that amount.\n\nThat is often more persuasive than a generic request for extra money.\n\n## Questions to ask before accepting\n\nAsk the recruiter these directly:\n\n### RSUs\n\n- On what date and using what share price is the $1.08 million converted into a fixed number of shares?\n- Is vesting monthly, quarterly, or annually?\n- Is there a one-year cliff?\n- Is vesting evenly distributed, or front-/back-loaded?\n- When is the grant formally approved?\n- What happens to unvested shares following an acquisition or termination without cause?\n\n### Bonus\n\n- What percentage of employees at this level received at least target bonus during each of the last three cycles?\n- What were the actual company-wide payout multipliers?\n- Is the first-year bonus prorated?\n- Are there company, team, and individual components?\n\n### Refresh grants\n\nRather than asking whether refreshes are \u201ctypical,\u201d ask for numbers:\n\n- When does a new employee first become eligible?\n- What was the median refresh grant for Senior PMs in each of the last two cycles?\n- What percentage of eligible Senior PMs received one?\n- Over how many years do refreshes vest?\n- Are they based primarily on performance, retention, market adjustment, or level?\n- Does a promotion grant replace or supplement the normal refresh?\n\nThey may decline to provide everything, but the specificity of their answers will tell you much more than \u201cwe have an annual refresh program.\u201d\n\n### Level\n\nConfirm that \u201cSenior Product Manager\u201d is the correct internal level:\n\n- What are the adjacent levels?\n- Is this an individual-contributor role?\n- What differentiates Senior PM from the next level?\n- Where does the offer sit within the compensation band for this exact level?\n\nA higher level can matter more than negotiating another $10,000 because it may affect future equity, refreshes, scope, and promotion timing.\n\n## Accept versus walk-away framework\n\nBecause you are already happy and were recruited, Halvern should pay a **switching premium**. Compare the offer against what you would realistically receive at Varrowby\u2014not just your current salary:\n\n1. Next 12 months of base\n2. Expected bonus\n3. Equity vesting during that period\n4. Any likely refresh or promotion\n5. Compensation you forfeit by leaving\n6. Commute, office attendance, workload, and role risk\n\nFor someone happy and not actively looking, I would personally want approximately a **15%\u201320% risk-adjusted improvement** in expected compensation, unless the new role is much better strategically.\n\n\u201cRisk-adjusted\u201d means not automatically valuing Halvern\u2019s $270,000 annual RSUs exactly like cash. Compare them consistently with your current employer\u2019s equity and consider both companies\u2019 volatility and outlook.\n\n## My recommendation\n\n1. **Negotiate.**\n2. Open with **$255,000 base, $1.3 million RSUs, and $75,000 sign-on**.\n3. Aim to land near **$250,000 base, $1.2 million RSUs, and $50,000 sign-on**.\n4. Count refresh grants as **$0** in your base-case decision.\n5. If they hold firm, do not reject automatically\u2014the current $552,000 target package is already strong.\n6. Accept only if it provides a meaningful premium over your actual next several years at Varrowby and the role itself is at least as attractive.\n\nThe most important missing information is your **current base, bonus, upcoming Varrowby vesting, and unvested-equity forfeiture**. Those numbers determine whether this is merely a strong market offer or a genuinely compelling reason for you to move.",
